# The NAS scripts are run by the Synology's busybox shell. A CRLF checkout (this
# repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true) would turn a
# shebang into "#!/bin/sh\r" and the script would stop running.
deploy/synology/compose text eol=lf
deploy/synology/install-shell text eol=lf
deploy/synology/update text eol=lf

# The backup/restore script runs in the postgres:16-alpine image's BusyBox
# shell (docs/specs/15-backup-restore-and-export.md), which has the same
# problem with a CRLF shebang. It is bind-mounted into the container straight
# from the checkout, so the checkout's line endings are the ones that run.
scripts/backup text eol=lf

# update_test.go writes the stub shell scripts it needs from Go raw string
# literals, so its own line endings end up in theirs. Go discards a \r inside a
# raw string literal, which would save it on a CRLF checkout anyway, but pinning
# the file is the version that does not depend on knowing that.
deploy/synology/update_test.go text eol=lf

# scripts/dev and its commands run under Git Bash, under the dev image's
# busybox sh (scripts/dev_test.go runs them there) and on a GitHub-hosted
# runner - the same CRLF shebang problem as the NAS scripts, and the same fix.
# dev_test.go is pinned for the reason update_test.go is.
scripts/dev text eol=lf
scripts/dev.d/* text eol=lf
scripts/dev_test.go text eol=lf
scripts/dev_packet_test.go text eol=lf

# gofmt's canonical output is always LF; on a CRLF checkout (Windows,
# core.autocrlf=true) `gofmt -l` reports every .go file as unformatted even
# when its content is otherwise correctly gofmt'ed, because the only
# difference is the line ending (H6, docs/plans/2026-09-harness-optimization.md).
# Pinning LF here is what makes `scripts/dev check` (which runs `gofmt -l`)
# usable from a Windows worktree at all. Existing worktrees need one
# `git add --renormalize -- '*.go' && git checkout -- '*.go'` (or a fresh
# checkout) to pick this up — the stored blobs are already LF (autocrlf=true
# converts on commit), so nothing in git history changes.
*.go text eol=lf

# scripts/doctor runs directly under sh (host, dev image busybox sh, GitHub
# runner) the same way scripts/dev does - same CRLF shebang problem.
scripts/doctor text eol=lf

# gate_test.go embeds gh/git stub scripts as raw string literals the same way
# dev_test.go and update_test.go do - same CRLF shebang problem, same fix.
# (Redundant with *.go above, kept as H5 wrote it rather than reflowed.)
scripts/gate_test.go text eol=lf

# agent-usage's fixtures are read as raw bytes (its awk does substring
# extraction, not JSON parsing), so a CRLF checkout would leave a trailing \r
# on every extracted field and break every hand-computed total in
# agent_usage_test.go. Redundant with *.go above for agent_usage_test.go
# itself (kept anyway, same precedent as gate_test.go just above) - the
# fixtures are not .go files, so they still need their own line.
scripts/testdata/agent-usage/**/*.jsonl text eol=lf
scripts/testdata/agent-usage/**/*.meta.json text eol=lf
scripts/agent_usage_test.go text eol=lf

# The runner container's entrypoint runs under bash inside a Linux image built
# from a Windows checkout (deploy/synology/runner/), so a CRLF shebang would make
# it "bad interpreter" — the same problem the NAS scripts above have, and the
# same fix. The Dockerfile is pinned too: its RUN lines use backslash
# continuations, which CRLF has broken in Docker builds before.
deploy/synology/runner/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
deploy/synology/runner/Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Same reason, same fix, for the agent runtime container (deploy/agent/, H18).
deploy/agent/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
deploy/agent/Dockerfile text eol=lf

# scripts/agent-loop.sh (H19, #318) runs under sh the same way scripts/dev and
# scripts/doctor do - same CRLF shebang problem, same fix. Its test file is
# sh too. scripts/package-prompt.template carries no shebang, but a CRLF copy
# would leave a trailing \r on every rendered prompt line when agent-loop.sh's
# sed-based renderer fills it in (Get-PackagePrompt reads the same file and
# is CRLF-tolerant, so only the sh side actually needs this pin).
scripts/agent-loop.sh text eol=lf
scripts/tests/agent-loop.test.sh text eol=lf
scripts/package-prompt.template text eol=lf
